# if / else

## Basic `if`

Go does not require parentheses `()` around conditions, but **requires** curly braces `{}`:

```go
temperature := 35

if temperature > 30 {
    fmt.Println("Hot weather!")
}
```

## if - else

```go
score := 75

if score >= 80 {
    fmt.Println("Passed with distinction")
} else {
    fmt.Println("Need to study more")
}
```

## if - else if - else

```go
score := 85

if score >= 90 {
    fmt.Println("Grade A")
} else if score >= 80 {
    fmt.Println("Grade B")
} else if score >= 70 {
    fmt.Println("Grade C")
} else if score >= 60 {
    fmt.Println("Grade D")
} else {
    fmt.Println("Grade F")
}
// Output: Grade B
```

## Short Init Statement — Important Go Idiom!

Go allows a short initialization statement before the if condition. Variables declared this way **only exist within the if-else scope**:

```go
// Common pattern: if err := ...; err != nil
if err := doSomething(); err != nil {
    fmt.Println("Error:", err)
    return
}

// Or with a value
if n, err := strconv.Atoi("42"); err == nil {
    fmt.Println("Parsed:", n)
} else {
    fmt.Println("Error:", err)
}
// n is not available here!
```

## Style Comparison

```go
// Without short init (more lines)
val, err := strconv.Atoi("123")
if err != nil {
    fmt.Println("Error:", err)
}
fmt.Println(val)

// With short init (more idiomatic, narrower scope)
if val, err := strconv.Atoi("123"); err != nil {
    fmt.Println("Error:", err)
} else {
    fmt.Println("Value:", val)
}
```

## Real-World Example: Input Validation

```go
func validateAge(age int) error {
    if age < 0 {
        return fmt.Errorf("age cannot be negative: %d", age)
    }
    if age > 150 {
        return fmt.Errorf("age is unreasonable: %d", age)
    }
    return nil
}

func main() {
    if err := validateAge(-5); err != nil {
        fmt.Println("Validation failed:", err)
    }

    if err := validateAge(25); err != nil {
        fmt.Println("Validation failed:", err)
    } else {
        fmt.Println("Age is valid!")
    }
}
```

## Tip: Avoid Excessive Nesting

```go
// Bad: deeply nested
func process(val int) {
    if val > 0 {
        if val < 100 {
            if val%2 == 0 {
                fmt.Println("Valid even number")
            }
        }
    }
}

// Good: early return / guard clause
func processClean(val int) {
    if val <= 0 { return }
    if val >= 100 { return }
    if val%2 != 0 { return }
    fmt.Println("Valid even number")
}
```

